[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the access switch (switch4) and its uplink to the routing switch (sshswitch), focusing on the two user segments (VLAN 5 and VLAN 6) reported as “down.” The L3 gateways for those VLANs live on sshswitch (10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254). From multiple hosts in both segments, gateway reachability is healthy: pc1 (10.5.0.1), pc2 (10.5.0.2) and dc_pc5 (10.5.0.5) all successfully ping 10.5.0.254; pc3 (10.6.0.3) and pc4 (10.6.0.4) successfully ping 10.6.0.254 (0% loss, low RTT). Cross-VLAN routing is also working (topology-wide reachability checks between segments succeed).

On the switching side, the uplink trunk is correctly carrying both VLANs: on switch4, eth0.5 → br_vlan5 and eth0.6 → br_vlan6, with access ports mapped appropriately (eth1–3,6 in br_vlan5; eth4–5 in br_vlan6). On sshswitch, br_v5 bridges bond0.5 and eth0.5 and hosts SVI 10.5.0.254; br_v6 bridges bond0.6 and eth0.6 and hosts SVI 10.6.0.254. No errors, drops, or link issues are observed on these interfaces.

Mechanism-wise, if the trunk tagging or uplink had been disturbed, clients would lose ARP/default-gateway reachability. Current state shows proper tagging and stable links, so the network is functioning. Root cause: there is no current fault; the earlier outage was likely due to transient maintenance (uplink/trunk temporarily mispatched or disabled) that has since been corrected. Corrective fix: no changes required now; recommend locking down access to the switch, documenting trunk VLANs (5,6), and monitoring to prevent ad-hoc changes during maintenance. 
=== END ===